WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.878
Hi, I'm Vellum!

00:00:02.878 --> 00:00:07.159
Broken certificate keys, two PHP bugs, a poisoned AI gateway release,

00:00:07.159 --> 00:00:08.715
and leaked platform secrets.

00:00:08.715 --> 00:00:10.272
Here's how they fit.

00:00:12.017 --> 00:00:15.492
The idea: rank by who is exploiting a bug and who can

00:00:15.492 --> 00:00:17.230
reach it, not by CVSS alone.

00:00:19.883 --> 00:00:23.308
A trusted root signs an intermediate, whose private key should be

00:00:23.308 --> 00:00:24.553
a huge random number.

00:00:24.973 --> 00:00:26.259
These keys were tiny.

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:29.794
Anyone could recompute one and sign a certificate for any site.

00:00:30.214 --> 00:00:32.000
Browsers pushed blocklists within hours.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.216
Software with its own trust store needs an update.

00:00:36.483 --> 00:00:39.877
The two PHP bugs sit in how a worker handles requests.

00:00:40.297 --> 00:00:43.603
A request arrives, and PHP sets up its internal state.

00:00:44.023 --> 00:00:46.099
An error stops the request part way through.

00:00:46.519 --> 00:00:50.271
Cleanup, or the next request on that worker, trusts memory that is

00:00:50.271 --> 00:00:51.522
half-built or already freed.

00:00:51.942 --> 00:00:53.987
The fixed releases reset that state.

00:00:53.987 --> 00:00:57.054
Restart every worker, or the old code keeps running.

00:00:58.817 --> 00:01:00.500
LiteLLM shipped a malicious release.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:01.846
Here is its path.

00:01:02.266 --> 00:01:05.984
Attackers held stolen developer credentials with release access.

00:01:06.404 --> 00:01:09.596
They pointed the release workflow at a lookalike repository.

00:01:10.016 --> 00:01:14.048
The wheels and Docker images, including latest, were out for about two hours.

00:01:14.468 --> 00:01:18.061
Planted files run whenever Python starts, so installing was enough.

00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:23.123
Sorting by CVSS puts the scariest worst case first.

00:01:23.573 --> 00:01:26.780
Sorting by risk asks: is it exploited, does it need a login,

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.116
can the internet reach it?

00:01:29.425 --> 00:01:30.584
The leak said ten.

00:01:30.584 --> 00:01:33.191
Both PHP bugs are published at nine point two.

00:01:36.433 --> 00:01:37.776
Update, restart, and rotate.

00:01:37.776 --> 00:01:41.134
A patched file protects nothing until the process is new.

00:01:41.134 --> 00:01:44.156
A leaked key keeps working until you rotate it.

00:01:45.567 --> 00:01:47.204
Weak keys break trust.

00:01:47.204 --> 00:01:48.841
Pin releases, rotate secrets.

00:01:48.841 --> 00:01:50.887
Rank by exploitation and reach.

00:01:51.307 --> 00:01:53.338
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:53.338 --> 00:01:53.918
I'm Vellum.

00:01:53.918 --> 00:01:54.208
Bye!
